      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment (part 2 of 2) of an OIML certificate.\n\nThe fragment contains:\n1. Certificate header info: Number R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03 revision 7, Project number 3954718, Page 5-7 of 7, Member State: The Netherlands\n2. Table 4: General characteristics of the indicating device (meter size vs indicating range vs verification scale interval)\n3. Footnote about DN600 display indication range\n4. Table 5: Approved software versions (across pages 5 and 6)\n5. Production location info\n6. Certificate history (revision history table)\n\nSince this is a fragment of one certificate, I should extract what's present:\n\n**certificate:**\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03\" (revision 7 — could note in revision)\n- scheme: \"A\" (from the number format R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03 — the \"A\" after year)\n- project_number: \"3954718\"\n- page_total: 7\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: not explicitly stated in this fragment... The revision 7 date is 3 October 2025 from revision history. Hmm, the certificate date_issued would typically be on page 1 which is not in this fragment. I could infer 3 October 2025 from revision history, but rule 8 says don't fabricate. The revision history shows revision 7 dated 3 October 2025, which corresponds to this revision. But date_issued is not explicitly in this fragment. I'll omit date_issued or... Actually, the safest is to omit it since it's not present in the fragment. Hmm, but the revision history shows the current revision date. Let me be careful: the fragment doesn't have \"Date issued\" text. I'll omit date_issued.\n\nActually, wait — maybe I should include it since revision 7 date 3 October 2025 is the date of this revision. But strictly, the field isn't in the fragment. I'll omit it to follow \"don't fabricate\". Hmm... Actually, one could argue the revision history defines it. But the instruction says \"If a field is not present in the markdown, omit it.\" The field \"date_issued\" is not pr