 DN200 till DN350: 9.999.999, VSI 0,01 → 0.01\n- DN400 till DN500: 9.999.999, VSI 0,1 → 0.1\n- DN600: 99.999.999*, VSI 0,1 → 0.1 (with footnote *)\n\nNote the number format uses dots as thousands separators (European style): \"99.999\" = 99999, \"999.999\" = 999999, \"9.999.999\" = 9999999, \"99.999.999\" = 99999999. And commas as decimal: \"0,0001\" = 0.0001, \"0,001\" = 0.001, \"0,01\" = 0.01, \"0,1\" = 0.1.\n\nWait — is \"99.999\" thousands or decimal? In Dutch convention, \".\" is thousands separator and \",\" is decimal. So 99.999 m³ = 99,999 m³ (ninety-nine thousand nine hundred ninety-nine). Verification scale interval 0,0001 m³ = 0.0001 m³. That makes sense for a water meter: DN50 indicating range 99 999 m³ with resolution 0.0001 m³. Yes.\n\nThe asterisk on 99.999.999* for DN600 references the footnote: \"* This display indication range is only available with software revision 2.0.52 or later...\"\n\nSo I should extract:\n- matrix_tables: \"General characteristics of the indicating device\" with columns meter_size, indicating_range, verification_scale_interval\n- model_family.models: DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600\n- characteristics.model_level: indicating_range (unit m³) and verification_scale_interval (unit m³) per model.\n\nSince this is only part 2 (pages 5-7), I should extract for content present in this fragment. The models here: DN50; DN65; DN80; DN100; DN125; DN150; DN200 till DN350 (DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350); DN400 till DN500 (DN400, DN450, DN500); DN600.\n\nHow to represent rows where multiple sizes share values? Options:\n- In matrix_tables rows: {\"meter_size\": \"DN65; DN80; DN100; DN125\", \"indicating_range\": 999999, \"verification_scale_interval\": 0.001}\n- In model_level: each model gets its value.\n\nFor model_level, I'd expand: DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125 each get 999999, 0.001. DN200-DN350 each get 9999999, 0.01. DN400-DN500 each get 9999999, 0.1. DN600 gets 99999999, 0.1 with footnote marker \"*\".\n\nWait DN600 indicating range has footnote *, so footnote_markers: [\"*\"] on the indicating_range value for DN600.\n\nHmm, actually should DN600's VSI also carry the footnote? The footnote says \"This display indication range is only available with software revision 2.0.52 or later...\" — the asterisk is on the indicating range only. So only that value.\n\nAlso the footnote mentions two conditions: software rev 2.0.52+ AND standard cover (IP68 cover not allowed for DN600). I'll capture the full text in footnotes[].\n\n**Table 5: Approved software versions** — this is a list of software versions with CRC checksums. Should this go into matrix_tables? Yes, it's a multi-dim table. It spans pages 5 and 6 — I should merge them into one table (same columns). Name: \"Approved software versions\". Columns: software_version, crc_checksum, remarks. Rows: each version with OTP CRC and APP CRC. The CRC checksum cell contains two values: e.g. \"bdFc (OTP CRC)<br>63b5 (APP CRC)\". How to represent? Maybe as object {\"otp_crc\": \"bdFc\", \"app_crc\": \"63b5\"}. That's nicer. Columns could be [\"software_version\", \"otp_crc\", \"app_crc\", \"remarks\"]. That transforms the table a bit but preserves info. Alternatively keep crc_checksum as a string. The schema says columns[] and rows[] (object per row). I think splitting into otp_crc and app_crc is cleaner and faithful.\n\nLet me list all software versions:\nPage 5: 9.5.28, 2.0.30, 2.0.33, 2.0.34, 2.0.35, 2.0.37, 2.0.38, 2.0.40, 2.0.42, 2.0.43\nPage 6: 2.0.44, 2.0.46, 2.0.50, 2.0.51, 2.0.52, 2.0.53, 2.0.54, 2.0.55, 2.0.56, 2.0.57, 2.0.58, 2.0.59, 2.0.60, 2.0.61, 2.0.62\n\nCRCs:\n- 9.5.28: OTP bdFc, APP 63b5\n- 2.0.30: OTP 0571, APP 1644\n- 2.0.33: OTP 0571, APP 7e5d\n- 2.0.34: OTP 0571, APP 6D40\n- 2.0.35: OTP 0571, APP E151\n- 2.0.37: OTP 0571, APP 019A\n- 2.0.38: OTP 0571, APP 6902\n- 2.0.40: OTP 0571, APP 7214\n- 2.0.42: OTP 0571, APP B459\n- 2.0.43: OTP 0571, APP 2191\n- 2.0.44: OTP 0571, APP 5ab6\n- 